Great problem. 

<details><summary>Solution</summary>Note that  $f$  is multiplicative (this is because  $\varphi$  is multiplicative). 

We now simplify the denominator. Note that  $n$  can be uniquely represented as  $2^km$ , where  $n$  is even and  $m$  is odd. Then,  $f(n) = f(2^k)f(m)$ . Fixing  $k$  and summing across all  $m$ , we find 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sum_{\nu_2{(n)} = k} f(n) & = & f(2^k)\left(f(1) + f(3) + \cdots\right) \mbox{.}
\end{eqnarray*}
Summing across all  $k$ , we find that 
\begin{eqnarray*}
f(2) + f(4) + f(6) + \cdots & = & \sum_{\nu_2{(n)} \geq 1} f(n) 
& = & \sum_{k \geq 1} \sum_{\nu_2{(n)} = k} f(n) 
& = & \sum_{k \geq 1} f(2^k)\left(f(1) + f(3) + \cdots\right) 
& = & \left(f(2) + f(4) + \cdots\right)\left(f(1) + f(3) + \cdots\right) \mbox{.}
\end{eqnarray*}
Therefore, the denominator factors as  $\left(f(2) + f(4) + \cdots\right)\left(f(1) + f(3) + \cdots\right)$ . 

Hence, the given fraction simplifies to  $\frac{1}{\sum_{k \geq 1} f(2^k)}$ . Noting that  $\varphi{\left(2^k\right)} = 2^{k - 1}$ , we get
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sum_{k \geq 1} f(2^k) & = & \sum_{k \geq 1} \frac{1}{\varphi{\left(2^{3k}\right)}} 
& = & \sum_{k \geq 1} \frac{1}{2^{3k - 1}} 
& = & 2 \sum_{k \geq 1} \frac{1}{\left(2^{3k}\right)} 
& = & 2 \frac{\frac{1}{8}}{\left(1 - \frac{1}{8}\right)} 
& = & \frac{2}{7} \mbox{.}
\end{eqnarray*} 
Thus, the desired fraction evaluates to  $\frac{1}{\frac{2}{7}} = \frac{7}{2}$ , giving an answer of  $\boxed{702}$ .</details>

