Notice we can factor the equations to get

\begin{align*}
(x-5)^2 &= 5(5-y)  
(y-5)^2 &= 5(5-z)  
(z-5)^2 &= 5(5-x)
\end{align*}

Now, make the substitution  $a=5-x$ ,  $b=5-y$ , and  $c=5-z$ . Then, we obtain another set of equations as follows:

\begin{align*}
a^2 &= 5b  
b^2 &= 5c  
c^2 &= 5a
\end{align*}

Solving, we see that  $c^7=5^7$  or  $c=0$ . Obviously, the sum of such  $c$  over all cases is  $0$ , so our desired sum is  $8(5-0)=\boxed{40}$ .

