Let  $H=\overline{AF}\cap\overline{BE}$  and  $D=\overline{CH}\cap\overline{AB},$  and note that  $H$  is the orthocenter of  $\triangle ABC.$  Hence,  $D$  lies on  $\omega_1,\omega_2$  and  $\overline{HD}$  is the radical axis of  $\omega_1,\omega_2.$  Since  $\overline{LN}\cap\overline{KM}=H,$   $(KNML)$  is cyclic. Since  $\overline{BC}$  is the diameter of  $(BKCM)$  and  $\angle CFK=90,$   $\overline{BC}$  is the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{KM}.$  Similarly,  $\overline{AC}$  is the bisector of  $\overline{KN},$  implying that  $C$  is the center of  $(KNML).$ Because  $\triangle HCF\sim\triangle BCD$  and  $\triangle BKC\sim\triangle KFC,$    $$ HC\cdot DC=FC\cdot BC=(KC)^2 $$   and the inverse of  $H$  with respect to  $(KNML)$  is  $D.$  We also know  $\overline{AB}\perp\overline{HD},$  so  $\overline{AB}$  is the polar of  $H.$  But  $\overline{ML}\cap\overline{NK}$  lies on the polar of  $H$  by Brocard, so it lies on  $\overline{AB}.$   $\square$

