My favorite problem of the contest :D

Let  $P'$  be the projection of  $P$  onto triangle  $ABC$ . Clearly  $PP'=15$ . Let  $x,y,z$  be such that  $x+y+z=1$ ,  $[P'BC]/[ABC]=x$ ,  $[P'CA]/[ABC]=y$ , and  $[P'AB]/[ABC]=z$ . Let  $h_A$  denote the length of the altitude from  $A$  to  $BC$ , and define  $h_B$  and  $h_C$  similarly; let  $d_A, d_B, d_C$  denote the lengths of the altitudes from  $P$  to  $BC, CA, AB$ , respectively; furthermore let  $K=[ABC]$  and use standard notation for the lengths of the sides ( $a=BC$  etc). By an area argument,  $d_A/h_A=x$ ,  $d_B/h_B=y$ ,  $d_C/h_C=z$ . By Heron's,  $K=84$ . 

The area of the base is constant, so we wish to minimize the area of the other three faces. The area we wish to minimize equals

\begin{align*}
[PBC]+[PCA]+[PAB] &= \frac a2\sqrt{d_A^2+15^2} + \frac b2\sqrt{d_B^2+15^2} + \frac c2\sqrt{d_C^2+15^2}
&= \frac a2\sqrt{h_A^2x^2+15^2} + \frac b2\sqrt{h_B^2y^2+15^2} + \frac c2\sqrt{h_C^2z^2+15^2}
&= \sqrt{\frac{a^2h_A^2}4\cdot x^2+\frac{a^2\cdot15^2}4}+\sqrt{\frac{b^2h_B^2}4\cdot y^2+\frac{b^2\cdot15^2}4}+\sqrt{\frac{c^2h_C^2}4\cdot z^2+\frac{c^2\cdot15^2}4}
&= \sqrt{K^2x^2+\frac{a^2\cdot15^2}4}+\sqrt{K^2y^2+\frac{b^2\cdot15^2}4}+\sqrt{K^2z^2+\frac{c^2\cdot15^2}4}
&= \sqrt{(Kx)^2+\left(\frac{15a}2\right)^2}+\sqrt{(Ky)^2+\left(\frac{15b}2\right)^2}+\sqrt{(Kz)^2+\left(\frac{15c}2\right)^2}
&\ge\sqrt{(Kx+Ky+Kz)^2+\left(\frac{15a+15b+15c}2\right)^2}
&=\sqrt{K^2+\left(\frac{15(a+b+c)}2\right)^2}
&=\sqrt{84^2+315^2}=21\sqrt{241}
\end{align*}
(The inequality step follows from vector triangle inequality: the norm of the sum of the vectors  $Kxi+\frac{15a}2j$ ,  $Kyi+\frac{15b}2j$ ,  $Kzi+\frac{15c}2j$ , is at most the sum of the norms, with equality iff the three vectors are parallel, i.e.  $x:y:z=a:b:c$ )
