\[X_i =  \begin{cases} 
      1 & \text{cookie  $i$  is directly eaten} 
     0 & \text{a superset of cookie  $i$  is eaten} 
   \end{cases}\]
Then, by linearity of expectation,
\[E[X_1 + X_2 + \cdots + X_{2^n - 1}] = E[X_1] + E[X_2] + \cdots + E[X_{2^n - 1}]\]
Now, if subset  $i$  is of size  $k$ , then there are  $2^{n-k}$  sets which are a superset of it, thus the chance it is eaten is  $\frac{1}{2^{n-k}}$ . So, we get that
\[E[X_1] + E[X_2] + \cdots + E[X_{2^n - 1}] = \sum_{k=1}^n \frac{1}{2^{n-k}}{n \choose k} = \boxed{\frac{3^n - 1}{2^n}}\]
and we are done.

This is equivalent to finding the expected number of cookies Kevin will eat. Now, the probability he will eat any one cookie labeled with  $C$  is just  $\left(\tfrac{1}{2}\right)^{n-|C|}$ . Hence, by Linearity of Expectation, the answer is just:
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=1}^n \binom{n}{k}\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^{n-k} &= \dfrac{1}{2^n}\sum_{k=1}^n \binom{n}{k}2^k 
&= \dfrac{1}{2^n}\cdot(1+2)^n-1 
&= \boxed{\dfrac{3^n-1}{2^n}}
\end{align*}
as required.  $\square$

