Firstly, by AM-GM, we know that   $$ 6ab \le 3a^2+3b^2 \implies \frac{1}{6ab+c^2} \ge \frac{1}{3a^2+3b^2+c^2}. $$   Therefore, it suffices to prove that   $$ \sum_{\text{cyc}} \frac{1}{a^2+2} \le \sum_{\text{cyc}} \frac{1}{3a^2+3b^2+c^2}. $$   Further manipulating the inequality, we get the equivalent inequality   $$ \sum_{\text{cyc}} \left(\frac{1}{a^2+2}-\frac{1}{3-2a^2}\right) \le 0, $$   which can be rewritten as   $$ \sum_{\text{cyc}} \frac{3x-1}{(x+2)(2x-3)} \le 0. $$   if we let   $$ x \coloneq a^2, y \coloneq b^2, z \coloneq c^2. $$   Employing the Tangent Line Trick on the function   $$ f(x)=\frac{3x-1}{(x+2)(2x-3)} $$   at  $x=\tfrac{1}{3},$  we see that   $$ f(x) \le -\frac{27}{49}x+\frac{9}{49} $$   for all  $0 \le x \le 1,$  so it follows that
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{\text{cyc}} f(x) &\le \sum_{\text{cyc}} \left(-\frac{27}{49}x+\frac{9}{49}\right) 
    &= -\frac{27}{49}+\frac{27}{49} 
    &= 0,
\end{align*}
as desired.  $\square$

