<details><summary>Solution</summary>Note that taking the reciprocal of both sides gives \begin{align*}\dfrac1{x_{n+2}}&=\dfrac{x_n^2+x_{n+1}^2}{2x_nx_{n+1}(x_n+x_{n+1})}&=\dfrac{(x_n+x_{n+1})^2-2x_nx_{n+1}}{2x_nx_{n+1}(x_n+x_{n+1})}=\dfrac{x_n+x_{n+1}}{2x_nx_{n+1}}-\dfrac1{x_n+x_{n+1}}.\end{align*}
Summing this from  $n=1$  to infinity gives \begin{align*}\sum_{n=1}^\infty\dfrac1{x_{n+2}}&=\sum_{n=1}^\infty\dfrac{x_n+x_{n+1}}{2x_nx_{n+1}}-\sum_{n=1}^\infty\dfrac1{x_n+x_{n+1}}\sum_{n=1}^\infty\dfrac1{x_n}-\dfrac1{x_1}-\dfrac1{x_2}&=\sum_{n=1}^\infty\dfrac{x_n+x_{n+1}}{2x_nx_{n+1}}-\sum_{n=1}^\infty\dfrac1{x_n+x_{n+1}}\sum_{n=1}^\infty\dfrac1{x_n}-33&=\sum_{n=1}^\infty\dfrac{x_n+x_{n+1}}{2x_nx_{n+1}}-\sum_{n=1}^\infty\dfrac1{x_n+x_{n+1}}.\end{align*}  Isolating the summation we want and watching magic happen gives \begin{align*}\sum_{n=1}^\infty\dfrac1{x_n+x_{n+1}}&=\sum_{n=1}^\infty\left(\dfrac{x_n+x_{n+1}}{2x_nx_{n+1}}-\dfrac1{x_n}\right)+33&=\sum_{n=1}^\infty\dfrac{x_n-x_{n+1}}{2x_nx_{n+1}}+33&=\dfrac12\sum_{n=1}^\infty\left(\dfrac1{x_{n+1}}-\dfrac1{x_n}\right)+33.\end{align*} The summation on the RHS is a telescoping series, so almost all the terms cancel out and we are left with a final answer of  $-\tfrac1{2x_1}+33=\boxed{23}.$</details>

We claim that the answer is  $\boxed{21}$  which is the number of primes between  $100$  and  $200$ . First we deal with all primes  $100 < p < 199$ . Here, say there exists  $r \not \equiv s \pmod{p}$  such that  $r^{11} \equiv s^{11} \pmod{p}$ . Now, 
\begin{align*}
    r^{11} &\equiv s^{11} \pmod{p}
    \left(\frac{r}{s}\right)^{11} & \equiv 1 \pmod{p}
\end{align*}
which implies  $\text{ord}_p(\frac{r}{s}) \mid 11$ . It is easy to list out all positive integers which are  $1 \pmod{11}$  between 100 and 199 and note that none of them are primes. However, Fermat's Little Theorem states that  $\text{ord}_p(\frac{r}{s})\mid 11$  and hence, we must have  $\text{ord}_p(\frac{r}{s})=1$  indicating that  $r \equiv s \pmod{p}$  which violates our assumption. Hence,  $0^{11},1^{11}, \dots , (p-1)^{11}$  are all distinct  $\pmod{p}$  implying that atleast one of these must satisfy  $x^{11} \equiv 2023 \pmod{p}$  so we simply consider  $y =0$  to obtain a valid solution.

Now consider the only remaining prime  $199$ . If there exists  $r \not \equiv s \pmod{199}$  such that  $r^{16} \equiv s^{16} \pmod{199}$  as before, 
\begin{align*}
    r^{16} & \equiv s^{16} \pmod{199}
    \left(\frac{r}{s}\right)^{16} & \equiv 1 \pmod{199}
\end{align*}
which implies  $\text{ord}_{199}(\frac{r}{s})\mid 16$  and by Fermat's Little Theorem  $\text{ord}_{199}(\frac{r}{s})\mid 198$  which since the latter is  $2 \pmod{4}$  implies that  $\text{ord}_{199}(\frac{r}{s}) \mid 2$  which since  $r \not \equiv s \pmod{199}$  implies  $r \equiv -s \pmod{199}$ . Hence,  $0^{16},1^{16},\dots , 99^{16}$  are all distinct  $\pmod{199}$ . Due to the even powers, these are also all quadratic residue  $\pmod{199}$  and since it is a set of  $100$  quadratic residues it must in particular be the set of all quadratic residues  $\pmod{199}$ .It now remains to confirm that  $2013 \equiv 23 \pmod{199}$  is a quadratic residue  $\pmod{199}$ .

By the Law of Quadratic Reciprocity, 
\[\left(\frac{23}{199}\right) = (-1)^{11 \cdot 99}\left(\frac{199}{23}\right)^{-1}= -\left(\frac{15}{23}\right)=1\]
since  $15$  is a non-quadratic residue  $\pmod{23}$  which is easy to confirm. Thus, there exists some integer  $y$  for which  $y^{16}\equiv 2013 \pmod{199}$  so setting  $x=0$  we have a pair of solutions in this case as well.

