We proceed by strong induction on  $n$ . The conclusion is clear for  $n=1$ . Now we proceed to the inductive step. Define  $g(n,k)$  to be the number of such functions.
Now,  $1,2,...,k$  can be sent to anything so that gives  $n^k$  different ways. Now, suppose  $i$  elements in  $k+1,...,n$  map to one of the first  $k$  elements. Its not too hard to see the number of ways this can happen then is:
\[
k^i \cdot \frac{g(n-k,i)}{(n-k)^i}
\]

EDIT: The proof of this is first select  $i$  elements  $n_1, n_2, ..., n_i$ . Now, each of these  $i$  elements may map to  $k$  different elements (namely,  $1$  through  $k$ ). Now, remark that the problem is effectively reduced to mapping each element in  $k+1$  through  $n$  such that for each element there exists some sufficiently high iteration of  $f$  it equals  $n_i$  for some  $i$ . The number of ways to count this is exactly  $g(n-k,i)$  as it doesn't matter whether or not we want every integer to reach  $[1,i]$  or  $\{n_1, ..., n_i\}$ , except that in computing  $g(n-k,i)$  the integers from  $1$  to  $i$  have  $n-k$  different selections to go to instead of  $k$ . Thus we must multiply by  $k^i/(n-k)^i$  to fix this.

Thus it follows:
\begin{eqnarray*}
g(n,k) & = & n^k \cdot \sum_{i=1}^{n-k} \dbinom{n-k}{i} \cdot k^i \cdot \frac{g(n-k,i)}{(n-k)^i} 
& = & n^k \cdot \sum_{i=1}^{n-k} \dbinom{n-k}{i} \cdot k^i \cdot \frac{i \cdot (n-k)^{n-k-1}}{(n-k)^i} 
& = & n^k \cdot \sum_{i=1}^{n-k} \dbinom{n-k-1}{i-1} \cdot \frac{n-k}{i} \cdot k^i \cdot \frac{i \cdot (n-k)^{n-k-1}}{(n-k)^i} 
& = & n^k \cdot \sum_{i=1}^{n-k} \dbinom{n-k-1}{i-1} \cdot  k^i \cdot \frac{ (n-k)^{n-k}}{(n-k)^i} 
& = & n^k \cdot \sum_{i=1}^{n-k} \dbinom{n-k-1}{i-1} \cdot  k^i \cdot (n-k)^{n-k-i} 
& = & n^k \cdot k \cdot \sum_{i=0}^{n-k-1} \dbinom{n-k-1}{i} \cdot k^i \cdot (n-k)^{n-k-1-i} 
& = & n^k \cdot k \cdot (n-k + k)^{n-k+1} 
& = & k \cdot n^{n-1} 
\end{eqnarray*}
as desired (this breaks on the case of  $k=n$ , but that's trivial).

