Let  $g(k,n-k)$  be the number of functional graphs such that there are  $k$  green vertices and  $n-k$  red vertices, each red vertex points to either a green or red vertex (no edge comes out of a green vertex) and there exists a path from any vertex to a green vertex. I claim  $g(k,n-k)=kn^{n-1-k}$  which obviously solves the problem.

Let  $S$  be the set of red vertices that point directly to a green vertex and enumerate  $1\le m=|S| \le n-k$ . Then 

\begin{align*}
g(k,n-k) &= \sum\limits_{m=1}^{n-k} \binom{n-k}{m} g(m, n-k-m) k^m 
&= \sum\limits_{m=1}^{n-k} \binom{n-k}{m}  m(n-k)^{n-k-1-m} k^m 
&= k\sum\limits_{m=1}^{n-k} \binom{n-k-1}{m-1} (n-k)^{n-k-m} k^{m-1} 
&= k \sum\limits_{m=0}^{n-k-1} \binom{n-k-1}{m} k^m (n-k)^{n-k-1-m} 
&= k (k+n-k)^{n-k-1} = kn^{n-k-1}
\end{align*}

