Let an integer  $m$  be called  $k$ - $\textit{free}$  if  $m$  is not divisible by any  $k$ th power. $\textbf{Claim:}$  We have that  $a_n$  is the  $n$ th smallest  $k$ -free positive integer.

We prove this by strong induction, with the base case of  $n=1$  being obvious. Then, assume that  $a_1,a_2,\dots,a_{n-1}$  are the smallest  $n-1$   $k$ -free positive integers, and let  $M$  be the smallest  $k$ -free positive integer greater than  $a_{n-1}.$ If  $a_{n-1}<x<M,$  we have that
\begin{align*}
1+\sum_{i}\left\lfloor\sqrt[k]{\dfrac{x}{a_i}}\right\rfloor&=\sum_i\sum_c 1_{a_ic^k\le x} 
&=1+\sum_i\sum_c\sum_{y\le x} 1_{a_ic^k=y} 
&=1+\sum_{y\le x}\sum_i\sum_c 1_{a_ic^k=y} 
&=1+\sum_{y\le x} 1 
&=1+x.
\end{align*}
Note that we have  $$ \sum_i\sum_c 1_{a_ic^k=y}=1 $$  because for any  $y$  there is a unique way of representing  $y$  as the product of a  $k$ th power and a  $k$ -free positive integer. Thus, we have that  $a_{n-1}\le x\le M$  does not satisfy the equation. However, if  $x=M,$  we have that
\begin{align*}
1+\sum_{i}\left\lfloor\sqrt[k]{\dfrac{x}{a_i}}\right\rfloor&=1+\sum_{i}\left\lfloor\sqrt[k]{\dfrac{x}{a_i}}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\sqrt[k]{\dfrac{x}{M}}\right\rfloor-1 
&=1+x-1 
&=x.
\end{align*}
Thus, we have that  $x=M$  does satisfy the equation. Thus, we have that  $a_n=M,$  which by the definition of  $M$  proves the claim by induction.

To finish, note that all primes are  $k$ -free for all  $k\ge 2.$  Combining this with our claim finishes the problem.

