<details><summary>Solution</summary>I claim that  $f(x), \ldots, f(x+3^n-1)$  are pairwise incongruent modulo  $3^n$  for all  $x,n \ge 1.$  The desired result is just the  $x=1, n=2013$  case. 

I proceed via induction. For the base case, we can easily take cases on  $f(x)$  modulo  $6$  to find that  $f(x), f(x+1),$  and  $f(x+2)$  are always pairwise incongruent. 

Now, assume that  $f(x), \ldots, f(x+3^k-1)$  are pairwise incongruent modulo  $3^k$  for a particular positive integer  $k.$  **<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>**  $f(x+3^k)-f(x) \equiv \pm \ 3^k \pmod{3^{k+1}}, \ \forall \ x \in \mathbb{N},$  under the inductive hypothesis.
*Proof.* Firstly, utilizing the given recursive relation, we get
\begin{align*}
    f(x+3^k) &= f(x+3^k-1)+2^{f(x+3^k-1)} 
    &= f(x+3^k-2)+2^{f(x+3^k-2)}+2^{f(x+3^k-1)} 
    &\cdots 
    &= f(x) + \sum_{i=0}^{3^k-1} 2^{f(x+i)}.
\end{align*}
Thus, it suffices to show that   $$ \sum_{i=0}^{3^k-1} 2^{f(x+i)} \equiv \pm \ 3^k \pmod{3^{k+1}}. $$   Note that all of  $f(x), \ldots, f(x+3^k-1)$  must be odd (it is easy to prove that  $f$  only takes on odd values using induction), so it follows that   $$ \{f(x), \ldots, f(x+3^k-1)\} \equiv \{1, 3, \ldots, 2 \cdot 3^k-1\} \pmod{2 \cdot 3^k}. $$   Since  $\varphi(3^{k+1})=2\cdot3^k,$  it follows by Euler's Totient Theorem that
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{i=0}^{3^k-1} 2^{f(x+i)} &\equiv 2^1+2^3+\ldots 2^{2\cdot3^k-1} \pmod{3^k+1}.
\end{align*}
By the geometric series formula, we have   $$ 2^1+2^3+\ldots+2^{3\cdot3^k-1} = \frac{2^{2\cdot3^k+1}-2}{3}, $$   so it follows by the Lifting the Exponent Lemma that
\begin{align*}
    \nu_3\left(\sum_{i=0}^{3^k-1} 2^{f(x+i)}\right) &= \nu_3\left(\frac{2^{2\cdot3^k+1}-2}{3}\right) 
    &= \nu_3\left(\frac{4^{3^k}-1^{3^k}}{3}\right) 
    &= \nu_3(3)+\nu_3(3^k)-\nu_3(3) 
    &= k.
\end{align*}
Hence, we can conclude that   $$ \sum_{i=0}^{3^k-1} 2^{f(x+i)} \equiv \pm \ 3^k \pmod{3^{k+1}}, $$   so we are done.  $\blacksquare$  

Now, continuing the inductive step of our proof, the hypothesis that  $f(x), \ldots, f(x+3^k-1)$  are pairwise incongruent modulo  $3^k$  tells us, using the lemma, that  $f(x), \ldots, f(x+2\cdot3^k-1)$  are pairwise incongruent modulo  $3^{k+1}.$  Then, applying the lemma to  $f(x+3^k), \ldots, f(x+2\cdot3^k-1)$  tells us that  $f(x+3^k), \ldots, f(3^{k+1}-1)$  are pairwise incongruent modulo  $3^{k+1}.$  

Now, to complete the inductive step, it remains to show that we cannot have   $$ f(x) \equiv f(x+2 \cdot 3^k) \pmod{3^{k+1}}. $$   For the sake of contradiction, assume that this is true. Applying the lemma for  $k+1,$  we know that   $$ f(x) \equiv f(x+3^{k+1}) \pmod{3^{k+2}} \implies f(x) \equiv f(x+3^{k+1}) \pmod{3^{k+1}}. $$   However, this means that   $$ f(x+2 \cdot 3^k) \equiv f(x+3^{k+1}) \pmod{3^{k+1}}, $$   violating the lemma, contradiction. 

Hence, by the Principle of Mathematical Induction, we have shown that  $f(x), \ldots, f(x+3^n-1)$  are pairwise incongruent modulo  $3^n$  for all  $x,n \ge 1,$  concluding the proof.  $\square$</details>

