Let  $g(n, k)$  be the number of ways to write  $n$  as a sum of exactly  $k$  powers of  $2$ . Then\[g(n, k) = [X^n](X+X^2+X^4+X^8+\cdots )^k.\]Since  $f(n) = \textstyle \sum_{k \ge 1} g(n, k)$ , we have\begin{align*}
\sum_{n \ge 1} f(n) X^n &=(X+X^2+X^4+\cdots)^1 + (X+X^2+X^4+\cdots)^2 + \cdots &=\frac{1}{1-X-X^2-X^4-\cdots} - 1
\end{align*}
We claim that  $f(n)$  is odd if and only if  $n = 2^m - 1$  for some positive integer  $m$ . It remains to prove that the coefficients of  $X^{2^m-1}$  in the generating function  $\textstyle \sum_{n \ge 1}f(n)X^n $  are all odd. Working in the field  $\mathbb{F}_2 [[X]]$ , this is equivalent to proving that
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{1-X^1-X^2-X^4-\cdots} - 1 = X^1 + X^3 + X^7 + X^{15} + \cdots \iff 1 = (1+X^1+X^3+X^7+\cdots)(1 + X^1+ X^2 + X^4 + \cdots ).\end{align*}
which is easy to see by a parity argument for each term. Thus our answer is  $2^{11} - 1 = 2047$ .

