Let  $\beta = \angle PXY$  and  $\alpha = \angle PYX$  and set  $XY = 1$ .  Observe that \[
	\angle AZX = \angle APX = \alpha + \beta 
	\] since  $APZX$  is cyclic.  In particular,  $\angle CXY = 90^\circ- (\alpha + \beta)$ .  It is immediate that \[
		BY = \sin \beta, \quad CY = \cos \left( \alpha + \beta \right), \quad AY = \cos \alpha, \quad AX = \sin \alpha.
\] The Law of Sines on  $\triangle XPY$  gives  $XP = XY \frac{\sin\alpha}{\sin(\alpha+\beta)}$ , and on  $\triangle XQY$  gives  $XQ = XY \frac{\sin\alpha}{\sin(90+\beta)} = \frac{\sin\alpha}{\cos\beta}$ .  So, the given is equivalent to \[
	\frac{\sin\beta}{\frac{\sin\alpha}{\sin(\alpha+\beta)}}
	+ \frac{\cos(\alpha+\beta)}{\frac{\sin\alpha}{\cos\beta}}
	= \frac{\cos\alpha}{\sin\alpha}
	\] which is equivalent to  $\cos\alpha = \cos\beta\cos(\alpha+\beta) + \sin\beta\sin(\alpha+\beta)$ .  This is obvious, because the right-hand side is just  $\cos\left( (\alpha+\beta)-\beta \right)$ .

