<details><summary>Solution</summary>Giving definition to an example would be hard, so we will show that we can find such a sequence.

First, let us extend the definition of \emph{territorial} to finite sequences. A finite sequence of positive real numbers  $a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n$  is called \emph{territorial} if for all positive integers  $i,j$  with  $i<j\le n$ , we have  $|a_i-a_j|\ge\frac{1}{j}$ . Now, let  $c$  be a real number with  $c\ge 2$ . Call a territorial sequence  $a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n$  or  $a_1,a_2,a_3,\ldots$  \emph{good} if  $a_i<c$  for any index  $i$ .

We will now show two important lemmas; the first is to be used in the second. For the lemmas, we will use special notation. For a sequence  $a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n$ , let the ordered sequence be  $b_1,b_2,\ldots,b_n$ . Also, let  $S$  be the set of all intervals of the form  $(b_k-\frac{1}{n+1},b_k+\frac{1}{n+1})$ , and  $S'=\overline{S}\cap(0,c)$ , where  $\overline{S}$  is the complement of  $S$ .

\begin{lemma}
For any finite good sequence  $a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n$ ,  $S'$  is non-empty.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Each interval of the form  $(b_k-\frac{1}{n+1},b_k+\frac{1}{n+1})$  has length  $(b_k+\frac{1}{n+1})-(b_k-\frac{1}{n+1})=\frac{2}{n+1}$ , and there are  $n$  such intervals, for a total length of  $\frac{2n}{n+1}$ . Clearly,  $2n<2n+2$ , and  $n+1>0$ , so by division,  $\frac{2n}{n+1}<2\le c$ . The length of  $(0,c)$  is  $c$ , so by definition,  $S'$  must not be empty.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
For any positive integer  $n$ , there exists a good sequence  $a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n$ .
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
We will prove the result using induction. For the base case, since  $1<2\le c$ , and the definition of \emph{territorial} does not apply, if  $a_1=1$ , then the sequence  $a_1$  is a good sequence.

Now assume that the result holds for a given positive integer  $k$ , so that  $a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_k$  is a good sequence.

To append a real number  $a_{k+1}$ , it is sufficient that for all positive integers  $i,j$  with  $i<j\le k+1$ , we have  $|a_i-a_j|\ge\frac{1}{j}$ , and for any index  $i$ ,  $a_i<c$ . We already have that for all positive integers  $i,j$  with  $i<j\le k$ , we have  $|a_i-a_j|\ge\frac{1}{j}$ , and for any index  $i$  which is not  $k+1$ ,  $a_i<c$ . So, it suffices to show that there exists a real number  $a_{k+1}$  with  $0<a_{k+1}<c$  and for any positive integer  $i$  with  $i\le k$ ,  $|a_i-a_{k+1}|\ge\frac{1}{k+1}$ .

For any  $x\in S'$ ,  $0<x<c$ . Also, for any positive integer  $j$  with  $j\le k$ , by definition of  $S$  and  $S'$ ,  $x\notin(b_j-\frac{1}{k+1},b_j+\frac{1}{k+1})$ . Since  $b_j=a_i$  for some  $i$ , for any positive integer  $i$  with  $i\le k$ , we have  $x\notin(a_i-\frac{1}{k+1},a_i+\frac{1}{k+1})$ , which means  $|a_i-x|\ge\frac{1}{k+1}$ . Then, we can let  $a_{k+1}=x$ . Hence, the result is true for  $n=k+1$ . By induction, we conclude that the result holds for all positive integers  $n$ .
\end{proof}

We will show using proof by contradiction that the maximum cardinality of all good sequence is infinite. Assume that it is finite, and let the maximum cardinality of all good sequences  $n$ . By Lemma 3, we can also find a good sequence with  $n+1$  terms. Therefore, the maximum cardinality is  $n+1$ , which is a contradiction. Therefore, the maximum cardinality of all good sequences is infinite, so we can find an infinite territorial sequence  $a_1,a_2,a_3,\ldots$  for which  $a_i<c$  for all  $i$ .

Or, we can take part of the induction argument for the proof of Lemma 3 and say that since we can always find another  $a_{n+1}$ , there exists an infinite sequence that satisfies the problem.</details>

