Let  $i$  be the non-negative integer such that  $\frac{i^2}{2} \le k < \frac{(i+1)^2}{2}$ . Note that, 
\begin{align*}
    n - \sqrt{2k} & \le n - \sqrt{2 \cdot \frac{i^2}{2}}=n-i 
    n+ \sqrt{2k} & \ge n+ \sqrt{2 \cdot \frac{i^2}{2}} = n+i
\end{align*}
which implies that the range  $[n-i,n+1] \subseteq [n - \sqrt{2k},n+\sqrt{2k}]$ . We show the stronger statement that there exists some player who won between  $n-i$  and  $n+i$  games (inclusive) via contradiction.

Consider the ideal situation where in every game the higher-rated player defeats the lower-rated one. Then, the number of wins for each player are as follows where  $P_1,P_2,\dots , P_{2n+1}$  are rated in descending order.
    \begin{tabular}{c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c}
        Player &  $P_1$  &  $P_2$  &  $\dots$  &  $P_{n-i+1}$  &  $\dots$  &  $P_{n}$  &  $P_{n+1}$  &  $\dots$  &  $P_{n+i+1}$  &  $\dots$  &  $P_{2n+1}$    
        \hline 
     Victories &  $2n$ &  $2n-1$  &  $\dots$   &  $n+i$   &  $\dots $  &  $n+1$  &  $n$  &  $\dots $  &  $n-i$  &  $\dots$  & 0 
    \end{tabular}

However, the players in the range  $P_{n-i+1}$  to  $P_{n+i+1}$  must win a different number of games as they all violate our assumption. Consider the player  $P_{n-i+j}$  in this range. In order for his number of victories to be outside the prohibited zone, he must either win  $j$  games against a higher-rated opponent or lose  $2i-j+2$  games against a lower-rated opponent. However, when  $1 \le j \le i$ ,  $j \le 2i-j+2$  and when  $2i \ge j > i$  ,  $ j \ge 2i-j+2$ . Also note that for each win against a higher-rated opponent there is a corresponding loss against a lower-rated opponent, so the total count of games with unexpected results is atleast, 
\begin{align*}
    \frac{1}{2} \left [(1+2+\dots + i) + ((i+1)+i + \dots + 1 ) \right ] & = \frac{1}{2} \left [ \frac{i(i+1)}{2} + \frac{(i+1)(i+2)}{2} \right ] 
    & = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{2i^2 + 4i+2}{2}\right)
    & = \frac{(i+1)^2}{2}
    & > k
\end{align*}
which is a clear contradiction. Hence there always must exist some player who won some number of games within the range  $[n-i,n+1]$  which in turn implies our result.

