For a tetrahedron \(ABCD\),      
- let \(I\) be the incenter of \(ABCD\);
- let \(I_A\) be the incenter of \(IBCD\),         with \(I_B\), \(I_C\), \(I_D\) defined similarly;
- let \(J_A\) be the incenter of \(I_ABCD\),         with \(J_B\), \(J_C\), \(J_D\) defined similarly; and
- let \(J\) be the incenter of \(J_AJ_BJ_CJ_D\).
 Observe that \begin{align*}     f(J)&=f(J_A)f(J_B)f(J_C)f(J_D)     &=f(I_A)f(I_B)f(I_C)f(I_D)\cdot(f(A)f(B)f(C)f(D))^3     &=f(I)^4\cdot(f(A)f(B)f(C)f(D))^6     &=f(I)^{10}. \end{align*}

By reflecting the entire diagram through the midpoint of \(\overline{IJ}\), we have \(f(I)=f(J)^{10}\), so \(f(I)=f(I)^{100}\), implying \(f(I)=1\). This is sufficient.

<span style="color:red">**Remark:**</span>      Note that we do not necessarily require \(I\ne J\).

